<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isma'ilism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ismaili, Ismailis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isma'ilism is a branch of Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> that recognises Ismail, son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> Ja'far al-Sadiq, and his descendants as the line of imams. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> was founded on Ismaili claims and promoted the faith through missionary activity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismailis</a> later opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, and their strongholds were destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> during the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ismailism.jpg" alt="Isma'ilism" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Md iet · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosque_Imam_Abadullah,Salamia,Syria.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Fatimid Caliphate grew not out of dynastic inheritance but out of a clandestine <b>Ismaili</b> missionary organisation, the da'wa, which spent decades preparing a revolution against the Sunni Abbasid order.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty were Sunnis of the Hanafi school who presented themselves as champions of the Abbasid caliphate, persecuting <b>Ismailis</b> in Multan and Shi'is and Mu'tazilites at Rayy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After suppressing a rising of the Khokhars in the Punjab, Mu'izz al-Din was assassinated in March 1206 at Damyak in the Punjab, either by <b>Isma'ili</b> agents or by Khokhars; the sources disagree.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The assassination of Nizam al-Mulk in October 1092, attributed to an <b>Ismaili</b> Nizari agent, was followed within weeks by the death of Malik Shah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamza ibn Ali ibn Ahmad, an <b>Ismaili</b> mystic and scholar from Zozan in Khorasan, came to Egypt in 1014 or 1016, gathered scholars who met in the Raydan Mosque near the Al-Hakim Mosque in Cairo, and in 1017 began to preach a Muwaḥḥidūn, or Unitarian, doctrine.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qarmatians, an <b>Ismaili</b> movement based in eastern Arabia, captured and looted the Abbasid city of Basra.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
